s (constant time).\n\nHowever, because the set of possible keys is much larger than the size of the array, two different keys will eventually produce the same index. This is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is how hash maps handle these collisions and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining (The most common method)\nInstead of storing the value directly in the array slot, each slot (or \"bucket\") points to another data structure\u2014usually a **Linked List** or a **Balanced Binary Search Tree**.\n* **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at index 5.\n* **Lookup:** The map hashes the key, goes to index 5, and then traverses the list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n* **Optimization:** Modern implementations (like Java 8+) convert the linked list into a Red-Black Tree if the list gets too long, keeping search times fast.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map searches for the \"next\" available empty slot.\n* **Linear Probing:** It checks $i+1$, then $i+2$, then $i+3$ until an empty spot is found.\n* *"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 28, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 7, "decoder_forward_passes": [24], "tokens_per_forward": [21.33333396911621], "prefill_seconds": 0.024485253999955603, "canvas_seconds": [0.265647113, 0.32645069400001603], "total_seconds": 0.6195681419999914, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 860.3843436344437, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 860.3843436344437, "seconds_per_step": 0.021252960285715567, "token_checksum": 26405514, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand why they happen. \n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an integer index in an underlying array. Because the number of possible keys is effectively infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually produce the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is how hash maps manage this problem and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Common Collision Resolution Strategies\n\nThere are two primary strategies used to handle collisions:\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining (Open Addressing)\nIn this method, each slot in the array does not store the data directly. Instead, it stores a pointer to another data structure (usually a **Linked List**). \n* **How it works:** If \"Key A\" and \"Key B\" both hash to index 5, index 5 will point to a linked list containing both entries. \n* **Lookup:** To find \"Key B,\" the map goes to index 5 and traverses the list linearly until it finds the entry with the matching key.\n* **Modern Variation:** Many modern implementations (like Java 8+) convert the linked list into a **Balanced Binary Search Tree** if the list grows too large, which keeps performance high.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available empty slot.\n* **Linear Probing:** If index 5 is taken, it checks 6, then 7, th"}]}
{"benchmark": "diffusion", "args": {"family": "diffusion", "model": "google/diffusiongemma-26B-A4B-it", "device": "auto", "dtype": "bfloat16", "compile": "auto", "expert_parallel": true, "experts_implementation": "by_token_count", "batch_sizes": [1, 4, 16, 2, 8, 32], "variants": ["grouped", "check1", "check0"], "prompts": "chat", "prompt_length": 64, "prompt_bucket": 64, "max_new_tokens": 512, "stop_check_interval": 4, "warmup_runs": 2, "timed_runs": 3, "gsm8k_examples": 50, "gsm8k_batch_size": 10, "seed": 0, "label": "diffusion_b4", "output": "/opt/dgemma/repo/results/session2/results.jsonl", "batch_size": 4, "sampler": "static", "cache": "static", "max_denoising_steps": null, "attn_implementation": null, "do_sample": false}, "environment": {"torch": "2.15.0+cpu", "transformers": "5.22.2", "python": "3.12.16", "device": "tpu", "timestamp": "2026-11-10T10:07:30Z", "torch_tpu": "unknown", "device_count": 1}, "load_seconds": 14.449297674999997, "warmup_seconds": [100.009086089, 3.444350190000023], "median_tokens_per_second": 719.8726845502558, "median_canvas_tokens_per_second": 734.1135479455529, "median_total_seconds": 2.853392735, "peak_memory_bytes": 20757802496, "runs": [{"num_denoising_steps": 64, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 16, "decoder_forward_passes": [27, 49, 43, 25], "tokens_per_forward": [18.962963104248047, 10.387755393981934, 11.906976699829102, 20.48000144958496], "prefill_seconds": 0.02324633000000631, "canvas_seconds": [1.6126930479999828, 1.5669840589999922], "total_seconds": 3.205234097000016, "generated_tokens": 2045, "canvas_tokens": 2048, "tokens_per_second": 642.6800320254009, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 643.6228389183477, "seconds_per_step": 0.04971855885937515, "token_checksum": 136006082, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, we first have to understand what a collision is.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an index (a number in an array). Because the set of possible keys is infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually map to the same index. This event is a **collision**.\n\nHere is how hash maps manage this situation and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Strategy: Chaining (Separate Addressing)\nThis is the most common resolution strategy. Instead of storing a single value at each array index, each index (or \"bucket\") points to a **linked list** or another data structure like a balanced tree.\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at index 5.\n*   **Lookup:** To find a value, the map hashes the key to find index 5, then traverses the linked list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n\n### 2. Strategy: Open Addressing\nIn this strategy, all elements are stored within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map \"probes\" for the next empty slot.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1^2, i+2^2$, and so on, to avoid \"clustering\" of data.\n*   **Double Hashing:** Use a second hash function to determine the step size for searching for the next slot.\n\n---\n\n### The Impact on Worst-Case Lookup "}, {"num_denoising_steps": 56, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 14, "decoder_forward_passes": [33, 39, 49, 33], "tokens_per_forward": [15.515151977539062, 12.102563858032227, 10.448980331420898, 15.515151977539062], "prefill_seconds": 0.03952784900002371, "canvas_seconds": [1.6112780079999993, 1.1753937490000226], "total_seconds": 2.828909976000034, "generated_tokens": 2008, "canvas_tokens": 2048, "tokens_per_second": 719.8726845502558, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 734.21277786799, "seconds_per_step": 0.049810395125000184, "token_checksum": 129024224, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand why they happen.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an index in an array. Because the number of possible keys is usually much larger than the size of the array, two different keys will eventually map to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is an explanation of how maps resolve these collisions and how it affects performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Common Strategies for Handling Collisions\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining (Open Hashing)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of storing a single value at each array index, each index (bucket) points to a **linked list** (or a balanced tree). \n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at index 5.\n*   **Lookup:** To find a value, the map hashes the key to find the index and then traverses the list until it finds the matching key.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing (Closed Addressing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next empty slot.\n*   **Linear Probing:** It checks the very next slot ($i+1, i+2, ...$).\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** It checks slots exponentially ($i+1^2, i+2^2, ...$) to avoid clustering.\n*   **Double Hashing:** Uses a second hash function to determine the \"step size\" for finding the next slot.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Impact on Worst-Case Lookup Time\n\nIn an ideal sc"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 56, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 14, "decoder_forward_passes": [29, 44, 50, 26], "tokens_per_forward": [17.65517234802246, 11.11363697052002, 10.24000072479248, 19.69230842590332], "prefill_seconds": 0.0636335680000002, "canvas_seconds": [1.4154768829999966, 1.3716699139999946], "total_seconds": 2.853392735, "generated_tokens": 2025, "canvas_tokens": 2048, "tokens_per_second": 725.8691086863987, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 734.1135479455529, "seconds_per_step": 0.04981712798214285, "token_checksum": 129888739, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, we first have to understand why they happen.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string or integer) into an index in an array. Because the number of possible keys is usually much larger than the size of the array, two different keys will inevitably map to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is how hash maps manage these conflicts and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n#### Separate Chaining (Most Common)\nIn this strategy, each \"bucket\" in the array does not store a single value. Instead, it stores a pointer to another data structure\u2014usually a **Linked List** or a balanced search tree.\n*   **How it works:** When two keys hash to the same index, the new entry is simply appended to the list at that index.\n*   **Lookup process:** To find a value, the map hashes the key to find the index, then traverses the linked list at that index until it finds the matching key.\n*   **Pros:** Simple to implement; the map never \"fills up\" (it just gets slower).\n\n#### Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this strategy, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map searches for the next empty slot using a specific pattern.\n*   **Linear Probing:** It checks the very next slot ($i+1, i+2, \\dots$).\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** It checks slots at an increasing interval ($i+1^2, i+2^2, \\dots$).\n*   **Double Hashing:** It uses a s"}]}
